==============================================================================
O23 — alignment replication  (mean-amplitude statistic, NOT a minima statistic)
==============================================================================
  B_N(t)    = sum_p p^(-1/2) sin(t log p)
  real_mean = mean(|B_N| at the true zeros)
  scr_mean  = mean(|B_N| at t ~ U(zeros.min(), zeros.max()))
  Z         = (real_mean - mean(scr_means)) / std(scr_means, ddof=1)

  replicating : /Users/juliansambrano/GitHub/primebeat/.archive/tests/suites/extreme_alignment_logging.py
  archived CSV: /Users/juliansambrano/GitHub/primebeat/archived_results/runs/primebeat_extreme_alignment_20251220_022922/results_extreme.csv
  O10 is a DELIBERATE GAP in this series and is not filled here.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 0 — prime_limits: VALUE bound or COUNT?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The original's build_primes_up_to (its lines 111-121) sieves an array of length
  N_max+1 and returns np.nonzero(sieve)[0] — the surviving INDICES, i.e. the prime
  VALUES <= N_max. No truncation to a count anywhere. Therefore prime_limits is a
  VALUE BOUND. PRIMARY convention here = 'value'.
  The COUNT reading is ALSO computed, as a SECONDARY contrast table.
  (The original's own comment at its line 128 assumes 25000 COLUMNS, i.e. the COUNT
   reading, while its code does the VALUE reading. Stated, not adjudicated.)

  Windows: the original masks on INDICES built as np.arange(1, len(data)+1)
  (its lines 100-109), and zeros1.txt is one column, so the windows are by ZERO
  INDEX, not by height t.

